Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their usage for this task  

• `stn` : station identifier ‑– filter to  723758  
• `year`, `mo`, `da` : parts of calendar date ‑– filter to  2009 / 10 and build a YYYY‑MM‑DD string  
• `temp` : mean daily temperature – aggregate (AVG) and sort to find the warmest days  
• (optional) `wban` : second station identifier – if the same USAF station appears under several WBAN codes we might want to average over them  
• Sentinel value check – `temp` = 9999.9 means “missing”, therefore we need the condition `temp < 9999.9`  

Conditions that will be used  

```
WHERE stn = '723758'
  AND year = '2009'
  AND mo   = '10'
  AND temp < 9999.9          -- throw away missing values
```  


Below are ten progressively‑more‑focused BigQuery SQL queries (all limited to 20 rows except the very last one) that lead to the final answer.

```sql
--Description: 1) Take a quick look at the 2009 table.
SELECT *
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.gsod2009`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 2) See which station numbers are present in 2009 (sample).
SELECT DISTINCT stn
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.gsod2009`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 3) Inspect a few rows for station 723758 in 2009.
SELECT *
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.gsod2009`
WHERE stn = '723758'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 4) Restrict to October (month = '10') for that station.
SELECT *
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.gsod2009`
WHERE stn = '723758'
  AND mo  = '10'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 5) Build a proper date string for those October rows.
SELECT FORMAT('%s-%02d-%02d', year, CAST(mo AS INT64), CAST(da AS INT64)) AS full_date,
       temp
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.gsod2009`
WHERE stn = '723758'
  AND mo  = '10'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 6) Count how many October rows have a missing temperature.
SELECT COUNT(*) AS total_rows,
       SUM(CASE WHEN temp = 9999.9 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS missing_temp_rows
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.gsod2009`
WHERE stn = '723758'
  AND mo  = '10';
```

```sql
--Description: 7) Preview valid temperature readings (exclude 9999.9).
SELECT FORMAT('%s-%02d-%02d', year, CAST(mo AS INT64), CAST(da AS INT64)) AS full_date,
       temp
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.gsod2009`
WHERE stn = '723758'
  AND mo  = '10'
  AND temp < 9999.9
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 8) Average temperature per calendar day (handles any WBAN duplicates).
SELECT FORMAT('%s-%02d-%02d', year, CAST(mo AS INT64), CAST(da AS INT64)) AS full_date,
       AVG(temp) AS avg_temp
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.gsod2009`
WHERE stn = '723758'
  AND mo  = '10'
  AND temp < 9999.9
GROUP BY full_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 9) Order those daily averages from hottest to coldest.
SELECT FORMAT('%s-%02d-%02d', year, CAST(mo AS INT64), CAST(da AS INT64)) AS full_date,
       AVG(temp) AS avg_temp
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.gsod2009`
WHERE stn = '723758'
  AND mo  = '10'
  AND temp < 9999.9
GROUP BY full_date
ORDER BY avg_temp DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 10) Final answer – top 3 hottest dates in October 2009 for station 723758.
SELECT FORMAT('%s-%02d-%02d', year, CAST(mo AS INT64), CAST(da AS INT64)) AS hottest_date
FROM `bigquery-public-data.noaa_gsod.gsod2009`
WHERE stn = '723758'
  AND mo  = '10'
  AND temp < 9999.9
GROUP BY hottest_date
ORDER BY AVG(temp) DESC
LIMIT 3;
```

Running the last query returns the three warmest October 2009 dates for station 723758 in the desired YYYY‑MM‑DD format.
